cite9†Mingquan Feng , cite10†Isabel Guan , cite11†Xuming Hu , cite12†Conghui He , cite6†Cunxiang Wang , cite13†Kexin Yang , cite14†Xingzhang Ren , cite15†Fei Huang , cite16†Dayiheng Liu , cite17†Linfeng Zhang L12: View a PDF of the paper titled Rethinking LLM Evaluation: Can We Evaluate LLMs with 200x Less Data?, by Shaobo Wang and Cong Wang and Wenjie Fu and Yue Min and Mingquan Feng and Isabel Guan and Xuming Hu and Conghui He and Cunxiang Wang and Kexin Yang and Xingzhang Ren and Fei Huang and Dayiheng Liu and Linfeng Zhang\nL13: \nL14: cite18†View PDF cite19†HTML (experimental) L15: > Abstract:As the demand for comprehensive evaluations of diverse model capabilities steadily increases, benchmark suites have correspondingly grown significantly in scale. Despite notable advances in redundancy reduction and subset-level performance prediction, a systematic framework that effectively integrates these methods to ensure both prediction accuracy and ranking consistency is still largely elusive.\nL16: In this paper, we first perform a sample-level analysis of benchmark redundancy and identify several highly similar samples that can be eliminated. Besides, we frame benchmark compression as an optimization problem with the aim of score reconstruction. Building on these, we then propose EssenceBench, a coarse-to-fine framework utilizing an iterative Genetic Algorithm (GA), which takes the advantages of fitness-based subset search and attribution-based sample search.\nL17: Compared to previous methods, our approach yields superior compression results with lower reconstruction error and markedly higher efficiency. In particular, on the HellaSwag benchmark (10K samples), our method preserves the ranking of all models shifting within 5% using 25x fewer samples, and achieves 95% ranking preservation shifting within 5% using only 200x fewer samples.\nL18: Comments:  | 18 pages, 5 figures\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Machine Learning (cs.LG)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite20†arXiv:2510.10457 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite21†arXiv:2510.10457v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite22†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10457†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Shaobo Wang [cite23†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Sun, 12 Oct 2025 05:38:10 UTC (1,351 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: ## Access Paper:\nL30: \nL31: View a PDF of the paper titled Rethinking LLM Evaluation: Can We Evaluate LLMs with 200x Less Data?, by Shaobo Wang and Cong Wang and Wenjie Fu and Yue Min and Mingquan Feng and Isabel Guan and Xuming Hu and Conghui He and Cunxiang Wang and Kexin Yang and Xingzhang Ren and Fei Huang and Dayiheng Liu and Linfeng Zhang\nL32: \nL33:   * cite18†View PDF L34:   * cite19†HTML (experimental) L35:   * cite24†TeX Source L36: \nL37: cite25†view license†creativecommons.org L38: ### Current browse context:\nL39: \nL40: cs.CL\nL41: \nL42: cite26†< prev |   cite27†next > L43: \nL44: cite28†new | cite29†recent | cite30†2025-10 L45: \nL46: Change to browse by:\nL47: \nL48: cite31†cs L49: cite32†cs.LG L50: \nL51: ### References & Citations\nL52: \nL53:   * cite33†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L54:   * cite34†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L55:   * cite35†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L56: \nL57: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL58: \nL59: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL60: \nL61: [Button: ×]\nL62: \nL63: loading...\nL64: \nL65: Data provided by:\nL66: \nL67: ### Bookmark\nL68: \nL69: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL70: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL71: \nL72: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL73: \nL74: Bibliographic Explorer (cite36†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL75: \nL76: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL77: \nL78: Connected Papers (cite37†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL79: \nL80: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL81: \nL82: Litmaps