mage: arXiv logo L14: \nL15: cite6† †www.cornell.edu L16: \nL17:  open search\nL18: \nL19: GO\nL20: \nL21: open navigation menu\nL22: ## quick links\nL23: \nL24:   * cite7†Login L25:   * cite4†Help Pages†info.arxiv.org L26:   * cite8†About†info.arxiv.org L27: \nL28: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL29: \nL30: arXiv:2510.10539v1 (cs)\nL31: \nL32: [Submitted on 12 Oct 2025]\nL33: # Title:Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions\nL34: \nL35: Authors:cite9†Yujie Ren , cite10†Niklas Gruhlke , cite11†Anne Lauscher L36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions, by Yujie Ren and 2 other authors\nL38: \nL39: cite12†View PDF cite13†HTML (experimental) L40: > Abstract:As large language models (LLMs) are increasingly applied in sensitive domains such as medicine and law, hallucination detection has become a critical task. Although numerous benchmarks have been proposed to advance research in this area, most of them are artificially constructed--either through deliberate hallucination induction or simulated interactions--rather than derived from genuine LLM-human dialogues.\nL41: Consequently, these benchmarks fail to fully capture the characteristics of hallucinations that occur in real-world usage. To address this limitation, we introduce AuthenHallu, the first hallucination detection benchmark built entirely from authentic LLM-human interactions. For AuthenHallu, we select and annotate samples from genuine LLM-human dialogues, thereby providing a faithful reflection of how LLMs hallucinate in everyday user interactions.\nL42: Statistical analysis shows that hallucinations occur in 31.4% of the query-response pairs in our benchmark, and this proportion increases dramatically to 60.0% in challenging domains such as Math & Number Problems. Furthermore, we explore the potential of using vanilla LLMs themselves as hallucination detectors and find that, despite some promise, their current performance remains insufficient in real-world scenarios.\nL43: Subjects: Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL44: Cite as: cite14†arXiv:2510.10539 [cs.CL]\nL45:   (or  cite15†arXiv:2510.10539v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL46:   cite16†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2510.10539†doi.org L47: \nL48: Focus to learn more\nL49: \nL50:   arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL51: ## Submission history\nL52: \nL53: From: Yujie Ren [cite17†view email ]\nL54: [v1] Sun, 12 Oct 2025 10:46:24 UTC (131 KB)\nL55: \nL56: Full-text links:\nL57: \nL58: ## Access Paper:\nL59: \nL60:  View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Hallucinations in Authentic LLM-Human Interactions, by Yujie Ren and 2 other authors\nL61: \nL62:   * cite12†View PDF L63:   * cite13†HTML (experimental) L64:   * cite18†TeX Source  L65: \nL66: cite19†view license L67: \nL68: Current browse context:\nL69: \nL70: cs.CL\nL71: \nL72: cite20†< prev |   cite21†next > L73: \nL74: cite22†new |  cite23†recent | cite24†2025-10 L75: \nL76: Change to browse by:\nL77: \nL78: cite25†cs L79: ### References & Citations\nL80: \nL81:   * cite26†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L82:   * cite27†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L83:   * cite28†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L84: \nL85: export BibTeX citation Loading...\nL86: \nL87: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL88: \nL89: ×\nL90: \nL91: loading...\nL92: \nL93: Data provided by:\nL94: \nL95: ### Bookmark\nL96: \nL97: cite29† †www.bibsonomy.org cite30† †reddit.com L98: \nL99: Bibliographic Tools\nL100: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL101: \nL102: Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL103: \nL104: Bibliographic Explorer (cite31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL105: \nL106: Connected Papers Toggle\nL107: \nL108: Connected Papers (cite32†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL109: \nL110: Litmaps Toggle\nL111: \nL112: Litmaps (cite33†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL113: \